#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078249 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078537 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eaac3fe1-285b-57ba-956b-66eaf51ded7e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Ratios Given a Triangle with Integer Side Lengths"}] :question/id #uuid "65c52289-9781-5418-b8c6-fb7693aad674" :question/math-academy-id "e-3240" :question/problem "Find $\\sin{\\theta}$, $\\cos{\\theta}$, and $\\tan{\\theta}$ for the triangle below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed11931671711d754241c84981e7cebf12f04bdae56a1c8b26a194a6bf44b7ca.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember the acronym \"SOH-CAH-TOA.\"\n\n- The sine of an angle is equal to the opposite divided by the hypotenuse (S-O-H). Relative to the angle $\\theta$, the opposite side is $\\overline{AC}$, and the hypotenuse is $\\overline{BC}$. Therefore,\n$\\sin{\\theta} = \\dfrac{AC}{BC} = \\dfrac{3}{5}$.\n- The cosine of an angle is equal to the adjacent divided by the hypotenuse (C-A-H). Relative to the angle $\\theta$, the adjacent side is $\\overline{AB}$, and the hypotenuse is $\\overline{BC}$. Therefore,\n$\\cos{\\theta} = \\dfrac{AB}{BC} = \\dfrac{4}{5}$.\n- The tangent of an angle is equal to the opposite divided by the adjacent (T-O-A). Relative to the angle $\\theta$, the opposite side is $\\overline{AC}$, and the adjacent side is $\\overline{AB}$. Therefore,\n$\\tan{\\theta} = \\dfrac{AC}{AB} = \\dfrac{3}{4}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078251 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078539 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6359c90-3e3e-54f3-a66b-c8f69058a3a3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Given a Trigonometric Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "e012dbd0-341b-5f01-a670-539c472471bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3243" :question/problem "Given that $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{4}$, find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/f8/f8affbac78c75000d7bbbc8c15508252f7bea0270fdf6cc9c5e5244eb89b505d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The tangent of an angle is equal to the opposite divided by the adjacent (T-O-A).\n\nRelative to the angle $\\theta$, the length of the opposite side is $x-1$, and the length of the adjacent side is $2x$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan θ &= \\frac{opposite}{adjacent} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4} &= \\frac{x - 1}{2x} \\\\\n2x &= 4(x - 1) \\\\\n2x &= 4x - 4 \\\\\n2x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078253 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078538 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c8132c-18c7-5250-a68e-68500bdb926e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio Given a Triangle with Non-Integer Side Lengths"}] :question/id #uuid "c9ac49b1-04df-5cf0-bd38-3bc5b044b612" :question/math-academy-id "e-3245" :question/problem "In the right triangle $\\triangle ABC$, the angle $\\angle A$ is a right angle, and the side lengths are $AB=2$, $AC=\\sqrt{5}$, and $BC=3$. Find $\\tan B$." :question/worked-solution "Let's illustrate the given triangle.\n\n![](images/4e/4e4e2005e31716a09953aed9054cc5796ee915d41d5a9020042984b348e3b743.png)\n\nThe tangent of an angle is equal to the opposite divided by the adjacent (T-O-A). Relative to $\\angle B$, the opposite side is $\\overline{AC}$, and the adjacent side is $\\overline{AB}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\tan{B} &= \\dfrac{AC}{AB} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078255 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078540 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcbd6c22-13de-5a61-90b0-38bd478b4f2f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Function Using a Calculator"}] :question/id #uuid "81f7e0c1-99bf-5feb-9db2-745595a8b613" :question/math-academy-id "e-3247" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\cos\\left(50^\\circ\\right)$. Round the answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (50^{∘}) &= 0.64278... \\approx 0.64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}]}
